2026-0708 / Make tool based on the MD5 history of a file, and not the
comparative ages of various files.


Like make, this tool will have a Makefile-like control file to list files to be
checked, and the action if the test passes. Unlike make, there won't be a
target with a series of filess, but rather a single target.

If the target doesn't exist, no action is taken.

If the index file doesn't exist, we note that and may create one later if
necessary. Or perhaps we attempt to read the index file into a data structure,
but if the index file isn't there, the data structure is left as undef.

If the target exists, but hasn't been seen before, a) we add the hash for the
file to the index, and b) we perform the action.

If the target exists, but the hash value matches the value on file, no action
is taken.

If the target exists, but the hash value is different than the one on file,
then the two steps a and b (above) are taken.


Arguments from the command line -- should follow the ones that make uses already.

-f Makefile

We'll also need to specify the location of the index file. I assume this file
would be in the same directory that the target file is in, but that may not
necessarily be the case, so we'll need a fallback, perhaps the home directory
for the current user.  For the first case, we can get away with just the
filename; in the second case, we'd need the full path name. The CWD may or may
not be a suitable location.

There may be issues when the permissions on the index file's directory changes,
so we might re-do some work. I'm going to assume that will be a rare edge case.
